🧠 2. Smart Pointers (VERY HIGH PRIORITY)
❓ Question
Explain unique_ptr, shared_ptr, and weak_ptr. When would you use each?
✅ Strong Answer
🔹 std::unique_ptr
Exclusive ownership
Cannot be copied, only moved
std::unique_ptr<int> p = std::make_unique<int>(10);
👉 Use when:
Single owner
Avoid overhead

🔹 std::shared_ptr
Shared ownership via reference counting
std::shared_ptr<int> p1 = std::make_shared<int>(10);
auto p2 = p1;
👉 Use when:
Multiple owners
Lifetime is shared
🔹 std::weak_ptr
Non-owning reference to shared_ptr
Prevents cyclic dependency
std::weak_ptr<int> wp = p1;

🔥 Staff-Level Insight (CRUCIAL)
“shared_ptr introduces atomic reference counting overhead, so in performance-critical systems, I avoid it unless ownership is truly shared.”
❓ Follow-up (VERY COMMON)
What is cyclic reference problem?
✅ Answer
Two shared_ptrs referencing each other → ref count never reaches 0 → memory leak
Fix:
Use weak_ptr in one direction

❓ Deep Question
How is shared_ptr implemented internally?
✅ Answer
Control block contains:
Reference count
Deleter
Uses atomic operations for thread safety

🧠 2. Smart Pointers — Deep Grilling
❓ Q4
Why is make_shared preferred over shared_ptr(new T)?
✅ Answer
Single allocation for:
Object
Control block
Better cache locality
Fewer allocations → faster
⚠️ Edge Case (IMPORTANT)
Cannot control custom deleter easily
May delay deallocation if weak_ptr exists

🔥 What they test
Optimization awareness
❓ Q5
What happens if two threads modify a shared_ptr?
✅ Answer
Reference count updates are thread-safe (atomic)
But object access is NOT thread-safe
👉 Important distinction:
“shared_ptr ensures safe ownership, not safe data access.”
🔥 What they test
Concurrency clarity

❓ Q6
Can shared_ptr cause performance issues?
✅ Answer
Yes:
Atomic ref count → expensive in multithreaded systems
False sharing on control block
Frequent increments/decrements
👉 Staff insight:
“In hot paths, I avoid shared_ptr and use unique ownership or object pools.”
🔥 What they test
Real-world performance thinking

❓ Q7 (Very Hard)
How would you implement a thread-safe reference counter without atomic?
✅ Answer
Use:
Thread-local counters + merge
External synchronization (mutex)
👉 But:
“Without atomics, correctness becomes complex and error-prone.”
🔥 What they test
Systems design thinking
